// Formative practice quiz for undoing-revert-and-reset. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/git-foundations/undoing-revert-and-reset

::undoing-revert-and-reset o1 - what a teammate sees after a revert::You pushed C12, which broke checkout, and your teammate already pulled it. You run git revert C12 and git push. Your teammate pulls again. What does their history show?{
=C12 is still there, followed by a new commit that undoes its change#Correct. git revert adds a new commit that applies the opposite of C12. History keeps C12, so the teammate simply pulls the fix on top.
~C12 is gone, as if it had never been made#Not quite. Revert never removes a commit. It adds a new one that undoes the change.
~C12 has been edited in place to remove the bug#Not quite. Existing commits are never changed by a revert. The fix arrives as a separate new commit.
~Their pull is rejected because the history was rewritten#Not quite. Revert does not rewrite history; it only adds a commit, so the teammate's pull goes through normally.
}

::undoing-revert-and-reset o2 - what --hard throws away::Your last commit C7 exists only on your computer. You run git reset --hard HEAD\~1. What happens to the changes C7 made to tracked files?{
=The branch moves back to the commit before C7, and those changes are thrown away from the staging area and working copy#Correct. --hard moves the branch back and discards staged and working-copy changes to tracked files.
~The branch moves back, and the changes wait, staged, in the staging area#Not quite. That is git reset --soft. --hard does not keep the changes.
~The branch moves back, and the changes stay in the working copy, unstaged#Not quite. That is the default, --mixed. --hard discards the changes instead.
~C7 stays on the branch, and only the working copy is cleaned#Not quite. Every git reset to HEAD\~1 moves the branch back, so C7 is no longer on it.
}

::undoing-revert-and-reset o2 - the default reset::You run git reset HEAD\~1 with no option to undo a local commit. Where do that commit's changes end up?{
=In the working copy, unstaged#Correct. With no option Git uses --mixed. The branch moves back and the changes stay in your files but are not staged.
~In the staging area, ready to commit again#Not quite. Keeping them staged needs --soft. The default leaves them unstaged.
~Nowhere; the default throws them away#Not quite. Only --hard throws changes away. The default keeps them in the working copy.
~Still in the commit, because reset with no option only prints what it would do#Not quite. git reset HEAD\~1 really moves the branch back. It is not a preview.
}

::undoing-revert-and-reset o3 - when reset is the right tool::In which situation is git reset --soft HEAD\~1 a safe way to undo the last commit?{
=You made the commit a minute ago and have not pushed it to anyone#Correct. Nobody else has the commit, so moving your branch back cannot make your history differ from anyone else's.
~You pushed it an hour ago and think nobody has pulled yet#Not quite. Once a commit is pushed, treat it as shared and revert it. A reset would make your history differ from GitHub's, and a normal push is rejected.
~You pushed it yesterday and a teammate already built on it#Not quite. Others already have the commit, so revert it instead. Resetting would make your history no longer match theirs.
~It is on the shared main branch and you want history to look tidy#Not quite. A tidy history is not worth breaking everyone else's. For commits others have, add a revert commit.
}
